// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
; specs/compiler/theory/warmup_t27b.t27 -- the t27b JIT warmup run of gHashTag/t27#7596, as data, classified by
; specs/compiler/theory/warmup.t27 (Barrett et al. 2017). TOOL-WRITTEN from the run files; do not edit by hand.
; ASCII only (L3).
; -- The run --
; Where: the Railway t27b lab (x86_64, 48 cores, shared), 2026-10-09 10:23Z-10:39Z, t27b built from master
; be9757784 plus the five-line `TIME <test> <ns>` print this PR adds to `t27b test --time` (cli/t27b/src/main.rs),
; cross-built for aarch64-linux and run as `qemu-aarch64 -L /usr/aarch64-linux-gnu t27b test w_<k>.t27 --time
; --quiet` (qemu 7.2, user mode). Nothing ran on the Mac.
; What: for each of the six kernels of specs/benchmarks (#6568), a file holding the kernel and 500 identical
; tests; t27b compiles the file once and runs the 500 tests one after another in one JIT image, so test i is
; in-process iteration i. 10 process executions per kernel, kernels interleaved (fib..f64loop, then again).
; Each iteration checks its result: fib(30) == 832040, sieve_reps(100) == 186200, matmul_checksum(200) ==
; 306804569, collatz_max_steps(30000) == 307, ternary_dot(3000) == 6892549, |leibniz(2000000) - pi| < 1e-6.
; These oracles pass `t27c test-report` (t27c's Zig backend) on a one-iteration file. All 60 executions
; exited 0 with 500 passed. The arrays below are the per-iteration times in microseconds (ns rounded).
; Caveats: (1) qemu-user translates aarch64 code to x86 on first execution and caches it, so an early slow
; iteration may be qemu's warmup and not t27b's; t27b itself compiles every function once before the first
; test and never recompiles (no tiers, no profiling). (2) The lab is shared: the 1-minute load average is
; recorded per execution below and was 21-88 on 48 cores. (3) 500 iterations, not the paper's 2000;
; warmup.t27 scales its window and steady-state length to match.
